<article class="env-lemma" id="0BUW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BUW">Lemma <span data-tag="0BUW">47.11.3</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a Noetherian ring, let $I \subset A$ be an ideal, and let $M$ a finite $A$-module with $IM \not= M$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
If $x \in I$ is a nonzerodivisor on $M$, then $\text{depth}_ I(M/xM) = \text{depth}_ I(M) - 1$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Any $M$-regular sequence $x_1, \ldots , x_ r$ in $I$ can be extended to an $M$-regular sequence in $I$ of length $\text{depth}_ I(M)$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Part (2) is a formal consequence of part (1). Let $x \in I$ be as in (1). By the short exact sequence $0 \to M \to M \to M/xM \to 0$ and Lemma <a href="/tag/0BUV" data-tag="0BUV">47.11.2</a> we see that $\text{depth}_ I(M/xM) \geq \text{depth}_ I(M) - 1$. On the other hand, if $x_1, \ldots , x_ r \in I$ is a regular sequence for $M/xM$, then $x, x_1, \ldots , x_ r$ is a regular sequence for $M$. Hence (1) holds. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>